  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 350 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery reveals a mid level shortwave \ntrough moving east-northeast through the Dakotas and Minnesota \namid quasi- southwesterly to zonal flow aloft. At the surface,\nan occluded front is working its way west to east. Seasonably \nrich moisture and even some instability resides east of the \nsurface front ahead of the shortwave trough. This is driving \nscattered light showers within the Red River Valley and \nMinnesota this afternoon. The window for additional \nthunderstorms in west- central MN has closed with instability \nalready being shunted away to the east.\n\nTonight, a reinforcing cold front will reintroduce low stratus\nto areas along and north of US Highway 2, with mostly clear\nskies elsewhere. This along with increasing surface high\npressure nosing into the region and lightening winds may allow\ntemperatures to drop into the mid 20s within portions of eastern\nND.\n\nThursday will be seasonably cool with highs in the 40s and dry \namid the post-frontal air mass. Starting Friday, ensemble \nguidance all points toward upper ridging building into the \nNorthern Plains. This will trend temperatures toward above \naverage, particularly by Saturday. The way this upper ridging \nbuilds may also promote low stratus, fog/drizzle - this lowers \nconfidence in exactly how warm temperatures get. Should low \nclouds/fog/drizzle linger, temperatures may be just slightly \nabove average with high temperatures in the 50s. However, if \nskies clear, temperatures could soar well into the 60s - \nespecially on Sunday into Monday.\n\nGetting into early to mid next week, ensemble guidance also \nhints at an upper shortwave trough in the Southwest to either \ntranslate or cut off eastward into the Southern and/or Central \nPlains. While most of this forcing looks to remain south of our \narea, there could be enough influence from this feature to bring\nthe next round of light rain into the forecast. With how warm\nantecedent temperatures will be ahead of this feature, as well \nas lack of colder air advecting behind it, rain would be the \nfavored precipitation type.",
